{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And these are the names, etc.</b> There is reason to note in the explanation of this count: (1) why was it necessary to say <b>Yaakov and his sons</b> — moreover it carries no meaning in the course of the narrative. (2) It says (Genesis 46:15) <b>all the souls of his sons, etc.</b> <b>thirty and three</b>, yet in their enumeration they are no more than thirty and two." "And our Rabbis of blessed memory sensed this and said (Genesis Rabbah, chapter 94) that it is Yocheved, who was born between the walls. And their words, of blessed memory, are to be received from their mouths and not from anyone else's, for there is no proof for this from the verse. Also they themselves said (Genesis Rabbah 94) that His Shechinah, may He be blessed, is what completes the seventy, and according to their words, of blessed memory, Yocheved is not included in the count — for otherwise they would be complete at the number seventy. And their words, of blessed memory, would be justified in the context of the verse (Genesis 46:27) that comes afterwards — ״all the souls coming, etc. seventy״ — not in this verse, for why should it count only the sons of Leah together, and if so what will it answer regarding thirty and three, which are no more than thirty and two? And as for the midrash that says ״this is Yocheved, etc.,״ it surely disagrees with the aforementioned midrash, and it is difficult for me also upon it, for according to his words — that he said Yocheved was born and through her the thirty-three sons of Leah were completed — if so, when the verse went back to include all the details of the counts nearby, it said (Genesis 46:26) ״all the souls coming to Yaakov, etc. sixty and six,״ and if Yocheved was born the tally would be sixty and seven, on this reckoning: thirty and three of Leah, sixteen of the sons of Zilpah — that makes forty and nine; eleven of the sons of Rachel, besides Yosef and his two sons who were in Egypt — that makes sixty; and seven of the sons of Bilhah — that makes sixty and seven. And why would the verse contradict itself and say ״all the souls, etc. sixty and six,״ when in detail it counts sixty and seven?" "Furthermore there is cause to wonder, for afterwards it went back and included Yosef and his sons, etc., and said ״all the souls, etc. seventy,״ and that is not so. True, according to the words of the midrash — that HaShem included His Shechinah among them — this verse works out, but the first verse remains difficult according to his words." "<b>And the correct reading</b> on the level of the plain sense of the verses is that the verse counts two tallies: in one it counts Yaakov, and in one it counts the seed of Yaakov. Therefore, when it includes Yaakov and his sons, they are seventy, and when it enumerates his sons without Yaakov, they are no more than sixty and nine." "Also in this the verses are precise regarding what we noted in them, for at the very beginning it showed us with a finger that it includes Yaakov in the first count, of the sons of Leah — that is, its saying <b>Yaakov and his sons</b>, meaning that it includes him within the scope of this count. And therefore it said at the end <b>all the souls of his sons and his daughters, thirty and three</b>, meaning all the souls mentioned in this passage together with his sons and his daughters, and he is together with them in the combined tally, and it was not necessary to mention him explicitly, relying on what it recorded at the beginning of the matter: ״Yaakov and his sons.״ Or perhaps for this reason it used the word <b>kol</b> (all), to include the father together with the sons in this count. And you will find that in the tally of the sons of Zilpah, which it gives nearby, it said (Genesis 46:18) ״and she bore, etc. sixteen souls״ and did not say ״all״; whereas in the count of the sons of Rachel (Genesis 46:22) it did say ״all״ — because it wished to include the sons of Yosef, through whom the tally reaches the stated sum. And as for its saying (Genesis 46:25) ״all״ regarding the sons of Bilhah, it intended to say that in all of them there are but seven — a small sum." "<b>Further</b>, you will find that for all three of the Matriarchs [i.e., the handmaids and Rachel] Scripture mentions the remembrance of childbirth: for Zilpah it says ״וַתֵּלֶד אֶת אֵלֶּה״ and so on; for Rachel it says ״אֲשֶׁר יוּלַּד לְיַעֲקֹב״; for Bilhah it says ״וַתֵּלֶד אֶת אֵלֶּה״ and so on — which it did not say for the sons of Leah. And according to our way, Scripture intends to alert you that not all thirty-three were born [by the mothers], and therefore if it had said ״וַתֵּלֶד אֵלֶּה לְיַעֲקֹב שְׁלֹשִׁים וְשָׁלֹשׁ״ the implication would have been that she gave birth to thirty-three, but that is not so — she gave birth only to thirty-two, and with the inclusion of their father together with his sons and daughters [they are] thirty-three." "And therefore, when it gives the final tally, it alerted you that the count of the sons and daughters on their own, apart from Jacob, does not come to that. That is its saying, in giving the details of the count, ״כָּל הַנֶּפֶשׁ הַבָּאָה לְיַעֲקֹב״ — it has thereby taken him out of the count. And it was precise in saying ״יוֹצְאֵי יֶרֶךְ יַעֲקֹב שִׁשִּׁים וָשֵׁשׁ״; and do not find difficult our question regarding [the verse that says] sixty-seven, for there it included Jacob and here it did not include him. And in combining the total number at the very end it said ״כָּל הַנֶּפֶשׁ לְבֵית יַעֲקֹב״ — meaning, he and his sons who came to Egypt [were] seventy." "<b>Also</b>, Scripture was wise to alert you to what we have explained, in that it was precise to vary its wording in two verses that come together regarding the two tallies: in the one it was precise to say <b>כָּל הַנֶּפֶשׁ הַבָּאָה לְיַעֲקֹב</b> — it placed the coming before the mention of Jacob; and in the second verse it said <b>כָּל הַנֶּפֶשׁ לְבֵית יַעֲקֹב הַבָּאָה</b> — it placed the mention of Jacob before the mention of the coming. By this it intended to say that in the first tally Jacob is not included, and therefore it said <b>הַבָּאָה לְיַעֲקֹב</b> — meaning, that came to him; from this you learn that he himself is not included. And in the second tally it said: all the souls belonging to the house of Jacob that came to Egypt and so on — from this you learn that Jacob did not go out of the count." "<b>And in order</b> not to contradict, heaven forbid, the words of our Rabbis of blessed memory who said that Yocheved was born between the walls — perhaps Scripture alluded to this in saying <b>כָּל נֶפֶשׁ וְגוֹ׳ וּבְנוֹתָיו</b>, and we find him [having] only one daughter; rather, this is Yocheved, who was born between the walls. But it did not include her in the count because Jacob is the one who completes [it], as I have written. And there is yet another reason why it did not include her in the count: since she was born between the walls she needed thirty days in order to exit the category of a nefel [stillbirth], and she was not fit to be counted among those who entered. And let it not be difficult in your eyes that our explanation is the reverse of the words of our Rabbis of blessed memory, for I have already informed you that regarding the plain sense of the Torah from which no halakhah is derived, permission is granted to an experienced student to innovate, and concerning them it is said (Song of Songs 5:16) ״חִכּוֹ מַמְתַקִּים וְכֻלּוֹ מַחֲמַדִּים״. And one can resolve the difficulty we raised according to their words, may their memory be for blessing, but it is forced, and the plain sense is like cast metal (Job 37:18) while the derash shall be expounded."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 46, :notes {5 "The reference to \"three Matriarchs\" appears to mean the three mothers other than Leah (Zilpah, Rachel, Bilhah), since his point is that Leah's birth records lack the childbirth formula."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:49:43.535108166Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}